WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.906 --> 00:00:04.239
- How do we know who we are?

00:00:05.475 --> 00:00:08.243
How do we know where
we exist in our brain?

00:00:08.243 --> 00:00:10.114
Our identity,

00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:14.114
something that everybody
knows but can't define.

00:00:15.158 --> 00:00:18.991
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:00:22.528 --> 00:00:24.595
I spend much of my day trying to answer

00:00:24.595 --> 00:00:27.752
these very, what used to
be philosophical questions,

00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:29.882
which are now biological questions.

00:00:29.882 --> 00:00:32.120
I am a cognitive neuroscientist

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.677
and I study the brain and all aspects

00:00:34.677 --> 00:00:36.642
of self-awareness, deception,

00:00:36.642 --> 00:00:39.675
and how people think about themselves.

00:00:39.675 --> 00:00:41.614
- [Narrator] Our expert
cognitive neuroscientist

00:00:41.614 --> 00:00:44.991
explores identity and
the mind with the help

00:00:44.991 --> 00:00:48.324
of the new Hulu original series, Chance.

00:00:51.055 --> 00:00:52.754
- In our initial studies,
we showed subjects

00:00:52.754 --> 00:00:54.650
their own face, and what we discovered

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:56.151
is that when you look at yourself,

00:00:56.151 --> 00:00:58.396
either in a picture or in a mirror,

00:00:58.396 --> 00:01:00.510
the neurons in the right prefrontal cortex

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.819
get very, very excited
and they start firing

00:01:02.819 --> 00:01:04.939
what we call action potentials.

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:07.342
When you remove that right hemisphere

00:01:07.342 --> 00:01:09.305
or temporarily disrupt it,
people have a very difficult time

00:01:09.305 --> 00:01:13.472
thinking about themselves or
reacting to their own face.

00:01:14.338 --> 00:01:16.216
We know that anywhere
in that frontal cortex,

00:01:16.216 --> 00:01:18.327
damage to those areas can cause

00:01:18.327 --> 00:01:21.244
a very profound personality change.

00:01:23.148 --> 00:01:25.629
- Jaclyn Blackstone, he struck you in

00:01:25.629 --> 00:01:28.481
the face with a jack handle.

00:01:28.481 --> 00:01:30.411
Shortly thereafter, you became aware

00:01:30.411 --> 00:01:34.231
of a second personality, Jackie Black.

00:01:34.231 --> 00:01:38.398
- [Jaclyn] She does things
that I don't want to do.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.575
- I would best describe a neuroscientist

00:01:42.575 --> 00:01:44.656
talking about dissociative
identity disorder

00:01:44.656 --> 00:01:46.080
as skeptical.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.867
We do know that it's an
often faked disorder.

00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:50.746
On the other hand, someone who suffers

00:01:50.746 --> 00:01:53.084
post-traumatic stress disorder often has

00:01:53.084 --> 00:01:56.634
a related dissociative
identity disorder with them.

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:58.891
The person goes back
to the traumatic event.

00:01:58.891 --> 00:02:01.224
(screaming)

00:02:02.398 --> 00:02:03.707
One of the findings in neuroscience

00:02:03.707 --> 00:02:07.389
is that whether you're faking
it or it's actually real,

00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:09.128
your brain doesn't really
know the difference.

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:12.539
People who are feigning
DID can actually fool

00:02:12.539 --> 00:02:14.540
the scanner into believing that

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.887
these are two different
personality states.

00:02:16.887 --> 00:02:19.911
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:02:19.911 --> 00:02:21.534
The self is very fluid and it shifts

00:02:21.534 --> 00:02:23.476
from situation to situation.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:27.838
It's very malleable,
and it's very adaptable.

00:02:27.838 --> 00:02:31.213
- You haven't been
yourself for a long time.

00:02:31.213 --> 00:02:32.716
- One of the discoveries in our lab

00:02:32.716 --> 00:02:35.360
is how much people don't
know about themselves,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.239
who they are, what they've done,

00:02:37.239 --> 00:02:39.134
who they're going to become.

00:02:39.134 --> 00:02:43.621
People are very inaccurate
when they evaluate themselves.

00:02:43.621 --> 00:02:45.041
Ingrained in every human being

00:02:45.041 --> 00:02:47.964
is some level of self-deception.

00:02:47.964 --> 00:02:49.804
Why that is is that it's
actually pretty healthy

00:02:49.804 --> 00:02:52.501
to not know who you are,
that the people who know

00:02:52.501 --> 00:02:57.308
who they are tend to suffer
from clinical depression.

00:02:57.308 --> 00:03:00.391
- [Woman] Look after yourself, Eldon.

00:03:02.695 --> 00:03:06.161
- Having a self is maybe
the most precious gift

00:03:06.161 --> 00:03:08.797
given to every human being,

00:03:08.797 --> 00:03:12.078
that we can actually
think about who we are,

00:03:12.078 --> 00:03:15.805
and I think that's what makes us human.

00:03:15.805 --> 00:03:19.722
(dramatic instrumental music)

